{{~##
   How a table file's bytes are read, and the key they are read with.

   Both are seams on the accessor rather than parameters, and for one reason: each is a
   decision the consuming project makes once and the generated code cannot make at all.

   **Nothing here knows about any engine.** The default reads a file with the BCL, and what
   an engine needs instead arrives by assignment - csharp-unity-adapter.sbn writes exactly
   that, and writes it into its own file.

   The seam is what makes the split possible, and it was here first: the validation host
   already fills the tables from memory by assigning to it. So this is not a new mechanism
   for Unity's sake, it is the one that was already load bearing.

   What the split buys is that this file, and every other file the C# target writes, is
   plain netstandard - which is what lets the same output be compiled into an assembly
   rather than shipped as sources. spec/validation/validation-usability-and-assembly-output.md.
##~}}
        public delegate Task<byte[]> ReadAllBytesAsyncDelegate(string filename);

        /// <summary>
        /// How a table file is read.
        /// </summary>
        /// <remarks>
        /// Replaceable, and that is the supported way to change it: assign your own before
        /// calling <c>ReadAllAsync</c> to read from a pack file, a CDN, an addressable, or
        /// anywhere else. The default below is chosen for the platform being compiled for
        /// and is correct for reading a file from disk.
        /// </remarks>
        public static ReadAllBytesAsyncDelegate ReadAllBytesAsync = DefaultReadAllBytesAsync;

        /// <summary>
        /// The key the table files were sealed with, or null when they were not sealed.
        /// </summary>
        /// <remarks>
        /// Beside <see cref="ReadAllBytesAsync"/> because it is the same kind of thing: one
        /// decision the consuming project makes once, at start-up, before the first table is
        /// read. A key threaded through every read signature would be the same value written
        /// out at every call site, and every call site is somewhere it could be wrong.
        ///
        /// Thirty-two bytes, the key the converter was given. Null - the default - is a
        /// project that does not encrypt, and it costs that project nothing: the load path
        /// opens the envelope either way, and a file that is not encrypted comes back from
        /// that unchanged. A project that does encrypt and leaves this null gets the reader's
        /// own message saying the file is encrypted and no key was given, rather than a
        /// failure somewhere inside the format.
        ///
        /// Where the key comes from is the one thing about this layer worth being careful
        /// with. It is a secret only until it reaches a repository, a build log or an issue,
        /// and it cannot be taken back afterwards - so it is read at start-up from wherever
        /// this project keeps secrets, not written into a source file. What the layer is
        /// for, and therefore what is and is not worth defending, is
        /// spec/wire/tcb-v104-composed-encodings.md section 4.
        /// </remarks>
        public static byte[] EncryptionKey = null;

        /// <summary>
        /// The key the table files were signed with, or null when they were not signed.
        /// </summary>
        /// <remarks>
        /// A different key from <see cref="EncryptionKey"/> and independent of it: a file can
        /// be signed without being encrypted, and encrypted without being signed.
        ///
        /// What it buys is the thing encryption does not. The cipher is a keystream XOR, so a
        /// bit flipped in the file is a bit flipped in the value it decrypts to - no key
        /// needed - and every structural check the format makes still passes, because a
        /// four-byte float accepts any four bytes. With this set, a file altered after export
        /// stops loading instead.
        ///
        /// Setting it makes the reader stricter in a second way: a file that carries no MAC
        /// is refused, because otherwise the check could be removed by zeroing sixteen bytes.
        /// So the order to turn this on in is data first, key second.
        /// spec/wire/tcb-mac-and-signature.md.
        /// </remarks>
        public static byte[] MacKey = null;

        /// <summary>
        /// Whether to check the MAC at all. True by default.
        /// </summary>
        /// <remarks>
        /// For tools that read exported files without the key, and for measuring what the
        /// check costs at load. It is not a security boundary - anyone who can flip a flag in
        /// a shipped binary can read <see cref="MacKey"/> out of the same binary.
        /// </remarks>
        public static bool VerifyMac = true;

        /// <summary>
        /// Reads a file from disk, which is what every plain .NET project wants and what an
        /// engine replaces by assigning to <see cref="ReadAllBytesAsync"/>.
        /// </summary>
        private static async Task<byte[]> DefaultReadAllBytesAsync(string filename)
        {
            return await File.ReadAllBytesAsync(filename);
        }
